

**0.94 × 0.94 ≈ 88%**

At 97% single-pass transmission:

**0.97 × 0.97 ≈ 94%**

So a three-point improvement in single-pass transmission can recover roughly six points of usable two-pass EUV energy.

## 4. Transmission becomes throughput

Connect this directly to Lesson 024.

If the resist needs a fixed exposure dose to suppress stochastic defects, any EUV energy absorbed by the pellicle has to be replaced with more source power, more exposure time, or both.

More exposure time means fewer wafers per hour.

So the tradeoff is:

**better mask protection can reduce contamination risk, but poor transmission can consume scanner throughput.**

Imec reported full-field carbon-nanotube pellicles with single-pass EUV transmission as high as 97% in scanner testing. In 2023, imec and Mitsui Chemicals announced a commercialization effort targeting CNT pellicles with at least roughly 94% transmission and durability for future high-power EUV systems.

The exact production specification will evolve. The economic relationship will not: when EUV is the bottleneck, every percentage point of transmission can matter.

## 5. Heat is another constraint

The EUV energy that does not transmit through the pellicle is not harmless.

Some of it becomes heat.

The membrane is extremely thin, yet it must survive repeated exposure without sagging, deforming, contaminating the mask or failing mechanically.

That becomes harder as source power rises.

The material therefore has to combine properties that do not naturally come together:

- very high EUV transmission;
- low reflectivity;
- mechanical strength;
- thermal stability;
- chemical cleanliness;
- long usable lifetime.

Carbon-nanotube membranes are attractive partly because they can combine high transmission with strength and high-temperature tolerance.

## 6. Why not simply run without a pellicle?

Because there is no free answer.

Without a pellicle, the scanner avoids the transmission penalty.

But the reticle is more exposed to contamination, so the fab needs stronger inspection, cleaning, handling and contamination-control procedures.

Cleaning an EUV mask is itself risky and expensive. Miss a particle and it can repeat into many dies; clean too aggressively and the mask can be damaged or its imaging behavior altered.

So the real comparison is not:

pellicle = safe, no pellicle = bad.

It is:

**which protection strategy minimizes total cost per good wafer?**

That includes scanner throughput, contamination probability, inspection frequency, cleaning risk, downtime and yield loss.

## 7. Why this matters economically

A pellicle is not likely to be the largest revenue pool in semiconductor equipment.

Its strategic importance can still be high because it protects a much more valuable production asset and a reusable master pattern.

This is a recurring supply-chain lesson:

**small dollar content can have large system value when failure propagates through an expensive bottleneck.**

ASML must make the scanner, mask handling and contamination-control system work with the pellicle. Mitsui Chemicals is developing EUV pellicles and is working with imec on carbon-nanotube designs. Mask inspection and process-control vendors matter because repeating reticle defects must be detected quickly.

The key investment distinction is between **importance** and **profit pool**. A component can be essential without becoming a huge standalone business.

